2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34973837260", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Émile Edmond Peynot was a prominent French artist sculptor and medallist.", "newValue": "Émile Edmond Peynot (1850–1932) was a preeminent French sculptor and medallist whose prolific output significantly shaped the public artistic landscape of Paris during the late 19th and early 20th centuries. A product of the rigorous academic tradition of the École des Beaux-Arts, Peynot distinguished himself early, becoming a key exponent of the Beaux-Arts style, which merged classical forms with a sense of national grandeur. His work, characterized by its monumental scale and allegorical themes, played a crucial role in adorning the architectural achievements of the Third French Republic, notably its grand exhibitions and civic infrastructure.

Peynot's career was marked by significant academic success and prestigious commissions. After winning the Grand Prix de Rome for sculpture in 1875, he honed his craft at the Villa Medici, immersing himself in classical antiquity. Upon his return to France, he contributed to some of Paris's most iconic structures. His notable works include 'Le Génie de la Guerre' (The Genius of War), one of the colossal equestrian groups for the Pont Alexandre III (1900), and numerous allegorical figures for the Grand Palais, built for the Universal Exhibition of 1900. He also created sculptural groups for the Opéra-Comique and the Hôtel de Ville, solidifying his reputation as a master of public monumental art.

